@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.2019 = private unnamed_addr constant [2310 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for classes that implement `__eq__` but not `__hash__`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AA class that implements `__eq__` but not `__hash__` will have its hash\0Amethod implicitly set to `None`, regardless of if a super class defines\0A`__hash__`. This will cause the class to be unhashable, will in turn\0Acause issues when using the class as a key in a dictionary or a member\0Aof a set.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aclass Person:\0A    def __init__(self):\0A        self.name = \22monty\22\0A\0A    def __eq__(self, other):\0A        return isinstance(other, Person) and other.name == self.name\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Aclass Person:\0A    def __init__(self):\0A        self.name = \22monty\22\0A\0A    def __eq__(self, other):\0A        return isinstance(other, Person) and other.name == self.name\0A\0A    def __hash__(self):\0A        return hash(self.name)\0A```\0A\0AThis issue is particularly tricky with inheritance. Even if a parent class correctly implements\0Aboth `__eq__` and `__hash__`, overriding `__eq__` in a child class without also implementing\0A`__hash__` will make the child class unhashable:\0A\0A```python\0Aclass Person:\0A    def __init__(self):\0A        self.name = \22monty\22\0A\0A    def __eq__(self, other):\0A        return isinstance(other, Person) and other.name == self.name\0A\0A    def __hash__(self):\0A        return hash(self.name)\0A\0A\0Aclass Developer(Person):\0A    def __init__(self):\0A        super().__init__()\0A        self.language = \22python\22\0A\0A    def __eq__(self, other):\0A        return (\0A            super().__eq__(other)\0A            and isinstance(other, Developer)\0A            and self.language == other.language\0A        )\0A\0A\0Ahash(Developer())  # TypeError: unhashable type: 'Developer'\0A```\0A\0AOne way to fix this is to retain the implementation of `__hash__` from the parent class:\0A\0A```python\0Aclass Developer(Person):\0A    def __init__(self):\0A        super().__init__()\0A        self.language = \22python\22\0A\0A    def __eq__(self, other):\0A        return (\0A            super().__eq__(other)\0A            and isinstance(other, Developer)\0A            and self.language == other.language\0A        )\0A\0A    __hash__ = Person.__hash__\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `object.__hash__`](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel.html#object.__hash__)\0A- [Python glossary: hashable](https://docs.python.org/3/glossary.html#term-hashable)\0A", align 1
