@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.2081 = private unnamed_addr constant [1226 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `for` loops that can be replaced with `yield from` expressions.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIf a `for` loop only contains a `yield` statement, it can be replaced with a\0A`yield from` expression, which is more concise and idiomatic.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afor x in foo:\0A    yield x\0A\0Aglobal y\0Afor y in foo:\0A    yield y\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Ayield from foo\0A\0Afor _element in foo:\0A    y = _element\0A    yield y\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as converting a `for` loop to a `yield\0Afrom` expression can change the behavior of the program in rare cases.\0AFor example, if a generator is being sent values via `send`, then rewriting\0Ato a `yield from` could lead to an attribute error if the underlying\0Agenerator does not implement the `send` method.\0A\0AAdditionally, if at least one target is `global` or `nonlocal`,\0Ano fix will be offered.\0A\0AIn most cases, however, the fix is safe, and such a modification should have\0Ano effect on the behavior of the program.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: The `yield` statement](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/simple_stmts.html#the-yield-statement)\0A- [PEP 380 \E2\80\93 Syntax for Delegating to a Subgenerator](https://peps.python.org/pep-0380/)\0A", align 1
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.2082 = private unnamed_addr constant [49 x i8] c"Replace `yield` over `for` loop with `yield from`", align 1
