@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.2084 = private unnamed_addr constant [1084 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for named assignment expressions (e.g., `x := 0`) in `assert`\0Astatements.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ANamed assignment expressions (also known as \22walrus operators\22) are used to\0Aassign a value to a variable as part of a larger expression.\0A\0ANamed assignments are syntactically valid in `assert` statements. However,\0Awhen the Python interpreter is run under the `-O` flag, `assert` statements\0Aare not executed. In this case, the named assignment will also be ignored,\0Awhich may result in unexpected behavior (e.g., undefined variable\0Aaccesses).\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aassert (x := 0) == 0\0Aprint(x)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Ax = 0\0Aassert x == 0\0Aprint(x)\0A```\0A\0AThe rule avoids flagging named expressions that define variables which are\0Aonly referenced from inside `assert` statements; the following will not\0Atrigger the rule:\0A```python\0Aassert (x := y**2) > 42, f\22Expected >42 but got {x}\22\0A```\0A\0ANor will this:\0A```python\0Aassert (x := y**2) > 42\0Aassert x < 1_000_000\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `-O`](https://docs.python.org/3/using/cmdline.html#cmdoption-O)\0A", align 1
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.2085 = private unnamed_addr constant [51 x i8] c"Avoid assignment expressions in `assert` statements", align 1
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.2086 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.2085, [8 x i8] c"3\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.2087 = private unnamed_addr constant [638 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `Decimal` calls passing a float literal.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AFloat literals have limited precision that can lead to unexpected results.\0AThe `Decimal` class is designed to handle numbers with fixed-point precision,\0Aso a string literal should be used instead.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Anum = Decimal(1.2345)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Anum = Decimal(\221.2345\22)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix Safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe because it changes the underlying value\0Aof the `Decimal` instance that is constructed. This can lead to unexpected\0Abehavior if your program relies on the previous value (whether deliberately or not).\0A", align 1
