@anon.9a3c7070530f84c5ad699604f23a6f35.2115 = private unnamed_addr constant [2357 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for \22dummy variables\22 (variables that are named as if to indicate they are unused)\0Athat are in fact used.\0A\0ABy default, \22dummy variables\22 are any variables with names that start with leading\0Aunderscores. However, this is customisable using the [`lint.dummy-variable-rgx`] setting).\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AMarking a variable with a leading underscore conveys that it is intentionally unused within the function or method.\0AWhen these variables are later referenced in the code, it causes confusion and potential misunderstandings about\0Athe code's intention. If a variable marked as \22unused\22 is subsequently used, it suggests that either the variable\0Acould be given a clearer name, or that the code is accidentally making use of the wrong variable.\0A\0ASometimes leading underscores are used to avoid variables shadowing other variables, Python builtins, or Python\0Akeywords. However, [PEP 8] recommends to use trailing underscores for this rather than leading underscores.\0A\0ADunder variables are ignored by this rule, as are variables named `_`.\0AOnly local variables in function scopes are flagged by the rule.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adef function():\0A    _variable = 3\0A    # important: avoid shadowing the builtin `id()` function!\0A    _id = 4\0A    return _variable + _id\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef function():\0A    variable = 3\0A    # important: avoid shadowing the builtin `id()` function!\0A    id_ = 4\0A    return variable + id_\0A```\0A\0A## Fix availability\0AThe rule's fix is only available for variables that start with leading underscores.\0AIt will also only be available if the \22obvious\22 new name for the variable\0Awould not shadow any other known variables already accessible from the scope\0Ain which the variable is defined.\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe.\0A\0AFor this rule's fix, Ruff renames the variable and fixes up all known references to\0Ait so they point to the renamed variable. However, some renamings also require other\0Achanges such as different arguments to constructor calls or alterations to comments.\0ARuff is aware of some of these cases: `_T = TypeVar(\22_T\22)` will be fixed to\0A`T = TypeVar(\22T\22)` if the `_T` binding is flagged by this rule. However, in general,\0Acases like these are hard to detect and hard to automatically fix.\0A\0A## Options\0A- [`lint.dummy-variable-rgx`]\0A\0A[PEP 8]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/\0A", align 1
